{"schema":1,"slug":"potawatomi-great-lakes-and-the-ohio-valley-wars","nation":"Potawatomi","baseRef":"/data/web/territories/_base-great-lakes.json","yearStart":1640,"yearEnd":1809,"presenceLabel":"Potawatomi town or homeland area (general)","presenceNote":"The Potawatomi homeland is the Great Lakes (Wisconsin, Michigan, northern Indiana and Illinois), outside Kentucky. The story itself has no dates; the page spans c.1640 (Green Bay and the Door Peninsula) to 1809 (Fort Wayne cession). Green Bay and Door to about 1700, then the St. Joseph River, Chicago and Milwaukee (low–medium confidence, c.1640–1763); the 1763–1809 areas are drawn as before (low confidence from 1763). The Kentucky link is the 1791 Scott and Wilkinson expeditions against Wea and Miami neighbours, and Potawatomi warriors in the Confederacy; the story names no Potawatomi raid on a Kentucky station.","confidence":"low-medium","approximate":true,"overlapNote":"Miami, Wea, Kickapoo, Ojibwe and Odawa (Three Fires), Illinois (their country was taken from the Illinois), Menominee and Ho-Chunk in Wisconsin. In the 1805 Grouseland treaty they acknowledged the Delaware right to sell; in 1809 they ceded with the Delaware, Miami and Eel River.","basis":"Illinois State Museum (Midewin exhibit), NHBP timeline, Indiana DNR, Forest County Potawatomi (Wisconsin DPI), Greenville and Fort Wayne treaty references.","basisChip":"ethnohistory","timeline":{"min":1640,"max":1810},"fadeYears":0,"fadeSouth":false,"view":{"full":"40 96 540 418","narrow":"40 96 540 418","card":"40 96 540 418"},"mapLabelPos":[-86.5,43.3],"pins":[],"startEra":{"potawatomi-fire-keepers-and-the-ohio-valley-wars":"homeland"},"stories":{"potawatomi-fire-keepers-and-the-ohio-valley-wars":{"yearStart":1640,"yearEnd":1809,"presenceLabel":"Potawatomi town or homeland area (general)","approxStart":true}},"legend":[{"kind":"soft","label":"Approximate general area of Potawatomi presence (whole counties, not a boundary)"},{"kind":"point","label":"Site (exact position)"},{"kind":"ring","label":"Site, location approximate"}],"eras":[{"id":"beaver","label":"c.1640–1700 · Green Bay and the Door Peninsula","short":"c.1640","yearStart":1640,"yearEnd":1700,"confidence":"low-medium","sources":["mpm_pot","ism_pot"],"storySlugs":["potawatomi-fire-keepers-and-the-ohio-valley-wars"],"text":"Nicolet met a few Potawatomi at Green Bay in 1634. Pushed west by the Iroquois in the 1640s–50s (the Beaver Wars), they were on the Door Peninsula by 1665 and, with French trade alliances, began to expand by 1670 (Milwaukee Public Museum; Illinois State Museum). The tint is an approximate general area (Door and Brown counties, Wisconsin, whole counties), not a boundary.","layers":[{"feature":"pot_gb","style":"soft"}],"apprTag":"Approximate general area · low–medium confidence","mapLabel":"POTAWATOMI","mapSubLabel":"approximate general area · c.1640–1700","mapLabelPos":[-88.0,45.7]},{"id":"sjoseph","label":"c.1700–1763 · St. Joseph River, Chicago and Milwaukee","short":"c.1700","yearStart":1700,"yearEnd":1762,"confidence":"low-medium","sources":["mpm_pot","dnr_in","nhbp"],"storySlugs":[],"text":"From about 1700 the Potawatomi moved back south along the west shore of Lake Michigan and into Michigan. By the 1760s there were Milwaukee and Chicago bands, villages on the St. Joseph River (Elkhart and St. Joseph counties, Indiana, and Berrien County, Michigan) and a Detroit band (Milwaukee Public Museum; Indiana DNR; Nottawaseppi Huron Band). The tint is an approximate general area built from whole counties, with Chicago and Detroit as approximate points. No source gives a boundary for these years.","layers":[{"feature":"pot_sj","style":"soft"},{"feature":"detroit","style":"point"},{"feature":"chicago","style":"point"}],"apprTag":"Approximate general area · Detroit and Chicago points approximate","mapLabel":"POTAWATOMI","mapSubLabel":"approximate general area · c.1700–1763","mapLabelPos":[-86.6,42.6]},{"id":"homeland","label":"c.1763–1795 · Southern Michigan, northern Indiana and Illinois","short":"c.1763","yearStart":1763,"yearEnd":1794,"confidence":"low","sources":["nhbp","dnr_in","ism_pot","greenville"],"storySlugs":["potawatomi-fire-keepers-and-the-ohio-valley-wars"],"text":"The Detroit Potawatomi left their river villages in 1763 and set up villages on the Huron River and the Raisin; Indiana DNR has Potawatomi moving as far south as the Wabash in the 1770s. By the 1800s there were over 100 villages, 14 of them in northern and central Illinois (Illinois State Museum). The tint is an approximate general area of southwest and southeast Michigan, northern Indiana and northeast Illinois, built from whole counties: no source gives a boundary. Potawatomi warriors stood in the Northwest Indian Confederacy; Kentucky’s 1791 columns under Scott and Wilkinson struck their Wea and Miami neighbours, not Potawatomi towns.","layers":[{"feature":"pot_home","style":"soft"},{"feature":"detroit","style":"point"},{"feature":"chicago","style":"point"}],"apprTag":"Approximate general area · Detroit and Chicago points approximate","mapLabel":"POTAWATOMI","mapSubLabel":"approximate general area · c.1763–1794","mapLabelPos":[-86.5,43.3]},{"id":"greenville","label":"1795–1809 · Greenville and the Chicago cession","short":"1795","yearStart":1795,"yearEnd":1809,"confidence":"medium","sources":["greenville","ebsco_fw","fcp"],"storySlugs":[],"text":"At Greenville (3 Aug 1795) the Potawatomi signed, took $1,000 a year and ceded six miles square at Chicago among the posts. On 30 Sept 1809 at Fort Wayne the Delaware, Potawatomi, Miami and Eel River ceded about 2.5 million acres along the Wabash (EBSCO; acreage varies by source, 2.5 to 3 million). The tint is an approximate general area, widened to northern and central Illinois and the upper Wabash where villages lay by the 1800s; it is not a boundary.","layers":[{"feature":"pot_later","style":"soft"},{"feature":"chicago","style":"point"}],"notDrawn":{"map":"POTAWATOMI HOMELAND: GREAT LAKES · NOT DRAWN","tag":"Homeland outside the map window; text only"},"apprTag":"Approximate general area · Chicago point approximate","mapLabel":"POTAWATOMI","mapSubLabel":"approximate general area · 1795–1809","mapLabelPos":[-87.3,43.3]}],"sources":[{"id":"ism_pot","label":"The Potawatomi (Illinois State Museum, Midewin exhibit)","url":"https://museum.state.il.us/exhibits/midewin/potowatomi.html"},{"id":"nhbp","label":"1763: Detroit Potawatomi leave Detroit (Nottawaseppi Huron Band of the Potawatomi)","url":"https://nhbp-nsn.gov/timeline/1763/"},{"id":"dnr_in","label":"Historical Indians of Indiana (Indiana DNR, PDF)","url":"https://www.in.gov/dnr/kids/files/historical-indians.pdf"},{"id":"fcp","label":"Forest County Potawatomi (Wisconsin DPI)","url":"https://dpi.state.wi.us/amind/tribalnationswi/fcp"},{"id":"greenville","label":"Treaty of Greenville, 3 Aug 1795 (Avalon Project)","url":"https://avalon.law.yale.edu/18th_century/greenvil.asp"},{"id":"ebsco_fw","label":"Treaty of Fort Wayne 1809 (EBSCO Research Starters)","url":"https://www.ebsco.com/research-starters/history/treaty-fort-wayne/"},{"id":"mpm_pot","label":"Potawatomi history (Milwaukee Public Museum)","url":"https://www.mpm.edu/educators/wirp/nations/potawatomi/history"}],"mainMap":{"general":"mm_general","points":"mm_points","labels":[{"text":"Potawatomi: approximate general area c.1640–1809 (Green Bay and Door, then the St. Joseph River, Chicago and Milwaukee, then Michigan, Indiana and Illinois). Low–medium confidence to 1763","at":[-87.2,43.6]}]},"geojson":{"type":"FeatureCollection","features":[{"type":"Feature","properties":{"id":"detroit","note":"Detroit (Wikipedia 42.3317, -83.0459). NHBP: the Detroit Potawatomi left their river villages in Oct 1763 and spread south and west; a 1771 deed at their ancient village is recorded.","approximate":true,"label":"Detroit","dx":9,"dy":5,"anchor":"start","name":"Detroit"},"geometry":{"type":"Point","coordinates":[-83.0459,42.3317]}},{"type":"Feature","properties":{"id":"chicago","note":"Chicago (Wikipedia 41.8819, -87.6278). Greenville Art. 3: six miles square at the mouth of the Chikago river where a fort formerly stood, and twelve miles square near the Illinois mouth, were ceded. Potawatomi ceded lands around Chicago later (the 1833 treaty is not covered here).","approximate":true,"label":"Chicago","dx":9,"dy":5,"anchor":"start","name":"Chicago"},"geometry":{"type":"Point","coordinates":[-87.6278,41.8819]}},{"type":"Feature","properties":{"id":"extent","kind":"main-map extent","approximate":true,"note":"Main-map extent: general areas (whole-county shapes, approximate), solid lines in Kentucky and sourced points."},"geometry":{"type":"GeometryCollection","geometries":[{"type":"MultiPolygon","coordinates":[[[[-89.8623,41.584],[-89.6324,41.5891],[-89.629,41.923],[-89.6854,41.9303],[-89.6885,42.1991],[-89.3986,42.2043],[-89.4014,42.5004],[-87.8005,42.4919],[-87.8286,42.2703],[-87.7417,42.1283],[-87.6715,42.0583],[-87.6117,41.8922],[-87.6092,41.8451],[-87.5761,41.786],[-87.5307,41.7482],[-87.5101,41.6963],[-87.4385,41.6707],[-87.4284,41.6459],[-87.278,41.6199],[-87.1183,41.6463],[-86.9356,41.7094],[-86.6791,41.845],[-86.598,41.9183],[-86.4852,42.1182],[-86.3566,42.2539],[-86.2739,42.4193],[-84.7185,42.4215],[-84.7061,42.0717],[-84.1399,42.0716],[-84.1311,42.4246],[-82.8704,42.4509],[-82.924,42.3521],[-83.0633,42.3179],[-83.0966,42.2899],[-83.1281,42.2377],[-83.1335,42.0881],[-83.1864,42.0657],[-83.1872,42.0076],[-83.2487,41.9726],[-83.2695,41.939],[-83.3155,41.9353],[-83.3416,41.88],[-83.3819,41.8706],[-83.438,41.8146],[-83.4242,41.7407],[-84.8033,41.6944],[-84.8029,40.9226],[-85.072,40.915],[-85.0685,40.5682],[-85.447,40.5669],[-85.4537,40.653],[-85.6386,40.6531],[-85.8597,40.651],[-85.8636,40.5655],[-86.3744,40.5614],[-86.3764,40.6906],[-86.5252,40.6924],[-86.5817,40.7358],[-86.585,40.9096],[-86.9268,40.9124],[-86.9864,40.8379],[-87.0974,40.8353],[-87.0984,40.7365],[-87.5226,40.7355],[-87.5264,40.4912],[-88.1179,40.4881],